en_US.aff/en_US.dic and nb_NO.aff/nb_NO.dic are PLACEHOLDER Hunspell-format
dictionaries, not real spell-checking word lists — each is a tiny,
hand-written set of common words in valid Hunspell `.aff`/`.dic` syntax
(see `src/spellcheck.rs`), authored directly for this repository with no
external sourcing, so they carry no licensing question of their own.

This exists to let the spell-check feature (GitHub issue #47) be built,
wired, and tested end to end — Settings, the Editor's inline underline
rendering, the caching, the tokenizer — without yet resolving which real
dictionary data to bundle. With only ~20 words known per language, nearly
every real word in a document will be (correctly, if uselessly) flagged
as unrecognized; this is expected and why `Settings::spell_check_language`
still defaults to `Off`.

Swapping in real dictionaries later is a drop-in replacement — same
`Dictionary::new(aff, dic)` loading path in `src/spellcheck.rs`, just
different bundled files. Do not treat that as done casually: a real
Hunspell word list is copyrighted work with its own license, and needs
checking for compatibility with smaragd's GPL-3.0-or-later before bundling.
Candidates investigated for issue #47 (English `en_US` and Norwegian
Bokmål `nb_NO`, both from JetBrains' `hunspell-dictionaries` GitHub repo,
also the source `spellbook`'s own test/example dictionaries use) ship with
a bare GNU GPL v2 `COPYING`/`license.txt` and no per-file "or any later
version" grant found anywhere in the bundle — GPL-2.0-only is NOT
automatically compatible with combining into a GPL-3.0-or-later work, so
this needs an explicit compatibility check (or a differently-licensed
source, e.g. an MPL/BSD-licensed dictionary) before real data replaces
these placeholders.
